    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-section-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 403,
      "sourceEnd": 432,
      "original": "### 2.1. Zeros of Polynomials",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0005",
      "sourceStart": 432,
      "sourceEnd": 434,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 434,
      "sourceEnd": 630,
      "original": "#### 2.1.1. Suppose $m$ is a positive integer and $p\\in\\mathcal{P}_m(F)$ . Suppose $\\lambda\\in F$. Then $p(\\lambda)=0$ if and only if there exists a polynomial $q\\in\\mathcal{P}_{m-1}(F)$ such that",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0006",
      "sourceStart": 630,
      "sourceEnd": 678,
      "sha256": "e485c8f925528c61827a45783d408a7a118494e6015d846fc77b02d8dc656407"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 678,
      "sourceEnd": 806,
      "original": "#### 2.1.2. Suppose $m$ is a positive integer and $0\\ne p\\in\\mathcal{P}_{m}(F)$. Then $p$ has at most $m$ distinct zeros in $F$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0007",
      "sourceStart": 806,
      "sourceEnd": 808,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 808,
      "sourceEnd": 942,
      "original": "#### 2.1.3. Suppose that $p,s\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$, with $s \\neq 0$. Then there exist unique polynomials $q,r\\in\\mathcal{P}(F)$ such that",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0008",
      "sourceStart": 942,
      "sourceEnd": 1264,
      "sha256": "093cd604c20ffcc9d791ce428765d03366cc3b02809dd9304e6d3262c9bdd1df"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 1264,
      "sourceEnd": 1358,
      "original": "#### 2.1.4. Every nonconstant polynomial with complex coefficients has a zero in $\\mathbb{C}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0009",
      "sourceStart": 1358,
      "sourceEnd": 1808,
      "sha256": "c8b83369257e14cdc8baf62e1a127c0383531b6de2ea845e46349a96a26320b4"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 1808,
      "sourceEnd": 1967,
      "original": "#### 2.1.5. If $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{C})$ is a nonconstant polynomial, then $p$ has a unique factorization (except for the order of the factors) of the form",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch04-block-0010",
      "sourceStart": 1967,
      "sourceEnd": 2161,
      "sha256": "0a51dd5d9fdcde84080512c5c3fdc211026ce208137773f5c20a71dcfade92d5"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch04-item-2-1-6",
      "sourceStart": 2161,
      "sourceEnd": 2318,
      "original": "#### 2.1.6. Suppose $p\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{C})$ is a polynomial with real coefficients and $p(\\lambda)=0$. Then $\\overline{\\lambda}$ is also a zero of $p$.",
